VERIFICATION
Every claim carrying a clause number or an attribution was checked against its
primary source before shipping. Several inherited claims did not survive: the
uppercase-tracking floor upstream states as required rests on a Bringhurst
passage about strings of small and full capitals, in which he writes that
display capitals have no generalised optimum; an easing curve attributed to
Material was wrong; and Arabic size and line-height ranges had no traceable
source and were removed.

The 2026-09-07 batch lost three more. Two attributed form-validation practice
to the ARIA Authoring Practices Guide: the APG has no form-validation or
error-summary pattern saying where focus goes after submit, and its spinbutton
example wires an invalid value with aria-errormessage rather than the
aria-describedby recipe. Both were replaced with the W3C WAI forms tutorial and
technique ARIA21, and the surviving GOV.UK half was narrowed to what that
design system does: it focuses the summary, and its examples do not use
aria-invalid at all. The third was a dated citation to a paywalled commercial
benchmark, supporting no rule a model could act on and uncheckable against a
primary source; it was deleted rather than hedged.

Reading a claim in an upstream file is not verification of that claim. The same
pass found two errors in OpenDesign's accessibility-baseline.md, which we did
not adopt: EN 301 549 V4.1.1 is described there as awaiting publication when it
is published (ETSI, 2026-09) and merely not yet cited in the Official Journal,
and Section 508 is called harmonised with EN 301 549 when the Access Board
expressly declined to incorporate it by reference.

The rules name no palette, because the output lands in the user's project
rather than in ours. Concrete hex values appear only as values to avoid.
